<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fort Duquesne</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1754">1754</a>–<a href="/years/1758">1758</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fort Duquesne was a French fort built in 1754 at the forks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio-river">Ohio River</a>, where the Allegheny and Monongahela rivers meet, on the site of present-day Pittsburgh. Its seizure from Virginian builders helped start the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-and-indian-war">French and Indian War</a>. The French blew it up and abandoned it in November 1758 as a British army under John Forbes approached, and the British built Fort Pitt on the site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fort-duquesne.jpg" alt="Fort Duquesne" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sébastien Paquin · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fortduquesne.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The forks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a> were considered strategically important for control of the Ohio Country, both for settlement and for trade. The English merchant William Trent had run a successful trading post there from as early as the 1740s, dealing with nearby Native American villages. The French claimed the area as part of the drainage basin of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>, and in the early 1750s built a line of forts: Fort Presque Isle on Lake Erie, Fort Le Boeuf, and Fort Machault on the Allegheny River.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Robert Dinwiddie, Lieutenant Governor of the Virginia Colony, saw these forts as a threat to the Ohio Company's claims. In late autumn 1753 he sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a> to ask the French to leave, and early in 1754 he sent Virginians to build Fort Prince George at the forks. Work began on 17 February; by 18 April a French force of five hundred under Claude-Pierre Pécaudy de Contrecœur had forced the small garrison to surrender. The French pulled down its fort and built Fort Duquesne, named after the Marquis Duquesne, governor-general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a>, on the model of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-frontenac">Fort Frontenac</a>.</p><p>The site was low, swampy and prone to flooding, and overlooked by highlands across the Monongahela. Contrecœur was preparing to abandon it as General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-braddock">Edward Braddock</a> advanced in 1755, but the British force was destroyed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-monongahela">Battle of the Monongahela</a>, with Washington serving as one of Braddock's aides. James Grant's attack in September 1758 was repulsed with heavy losses. On 25 November 1758 the Forbes Expedition under General John Forbes took possession of the site, which the French had destroyed and abandoned.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The point where the rivers meet, in Downtown Pittsburgh, has been preserved since the late 20th century as Point State Park, where the outlines of Fort Duquesne and of Fort Pitt, built by the British between 1759 and 1761, are marked on the ground. Excavations in 2007 found a brick drain and a refilled pit that may belong to the fort. The 200th anniversary of its capture was marked on 25 November 1958 with a commemorative stamp.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fort Duquesne” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Conflict over the Ohio country, where the French built <b>Fort Duquesne</b> in 1754, opened the final war.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-france">New France · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
